    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0041",
      "sequence": 53,
      "meaning": "The charming story of Sītā’s self-choice ceremony spreads beauty over the lovely river. The many skilful questions are its boats, and discerning answers its able boatmen. Hearing the mutual retellings, gatherings of travellers adorn this river. Bhṛgunātha’s rage is its fierce current, and Rāma’s excellent words its well-constructed ghāṭs. The festive joy of Rāma’s wedding with his brothers is an auspicious surge that delights everyone. Meritorious people who rejoice and thrill while telling and hearing it bathe with gladdened minds. The auspicious preparations for Rāma’s coronation are like communities assembled on a festival conjunction. Kaikeyī’s corrupt understanding is algae whose result was dense calamity. Bharata’s life is recitation and sacrifice that calms countless disturbances, while accounts of Kali’s sins and the wicked’s faults are the river’s dirt, cranes and crows.",
