    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0043",
      "sequence": 55,
      "meaning": "My distress, petition, humility and littleness form no small amount of lovely, excellent water. This wondrous water, beneficial when heard, removes hope, thirst and the mind’s impurity. It nourishes supreme love for Rāma and removes every fatigue caused by Kali’s stain. It dries the exhaustion of worldly existence, gives satisfaction to contentment, and calms wrongdoing, suffering, poverty and fault. It destroys desire, anger, pride and delusion and increases stainless discernment and detachment. By reverently bathing in and drinking it, sins and heart’s torments disappear. Those who do not wash their minds in this water are cowards ruined by the age of Kali. Thirsty and seeing the sun’s rays in the water-like world, those suffering creatures wander like deer chasing a mirage. Counting the virtues of this fine water according to my understanding, I bathe the mind. Remembering Bhavānī and Śaṅkara, the poet tells the lovely story. Now, holding Raghupati’s lotus feet in my heart and receiving grace, I tell the beautiful dialogue at the meeting of the two eminent sages.",
