    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0044",
      "sequence": 56,
      "meaning": "The sage Bharadvāja lived at Prayāga and held profound love for Rāma’s feet. An ascetic, treasure of equanimity, self-control and compassion, he was supremely wise in the path of highest truth. When the sun enters Capricorn in Māgha, everyone comes to the king of sacred places. Hosts of gods, demons, kinnaras and humans reverently bathe at the entire Triveṇī. They worship Mādhava’s lotus feet and, touching the imperishable banyan, their bodies thrill with joy. Bharadvāja’s hermitage was exceedingly holy, supremely delightful and beloved of eminent sages. There gathered communities of sages and seers who had gone to bathe at the king of pilgrimages. They bathed at dawn with joyful enthusiasm and told one another Hari’s manifold virtues. They explained Brahman, the principles of dharma and divisions of truth, and spoke of devotion to the Lord joined with knowledge and detachment.",
