    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0045",
      "sequence": 57,
      "meaning": "Thus they bathed throughout Māgha and then each returned to his own hermitage. Every year there was great joy as companies of sages bathed during Capricorn and departed. Once, after bathing through the Capricorn period, all the great sages set out for their hermitages. Bharadvāja, however, bowed at the feet of the supremely discerning sage Yājñavalkya and kept him back. Reverently he washed the sage’s lotus feet and seated him on a deeply sanctified seat. After worship and praise of the sage’s fair renown, he spoke in very pure, gentle words: ‘Master, I have one great doubt, while the entire truth of the Veda rests in your hand. I feel fear and shame in voicing it, yet if I do not speak, great harm will result. O Lord, saints declare this principle and Veda, Purāṇa and sages sing it: pure discernment does not arise in the heart when one conceals something from the teacher.’",
